     "marks": 2,
     "stem": "Use Figure 4 to give the time at which the semilunar valve in the aorta opens and the time at which it closes. Explain how you used the figure to identify these times.",
     "scheme": [
      {
       "point": "Opens at 0.14 to 0.16 s and closes at 0.39 to 0.43 s (both needed).",
       "marks": 1
      },
      {
       "point": "The valve opens when the pressure in the left ventricle becomes higher than the pressure in the aorta, and closes when the pressure in the ventricle falls below the pressure in the aorta (the points where the two curves cross).",
       "marks": 1
      }
     ],
     "alternatives": "The ventricle and aorta curves run very close together just before and after each crossing, so accept any time from 0.14 to 0.16 s for opening and any time from 0.39 to 0.43 s for closing (a reading taken at the small dip in the aortic curve, about 0.42 s, is correct: the dip is caused by the valve closing). Accept 'where the ventricle and aorta lines cross' for the second mark only if it is clear which crossing is the opening and which is the closing, or the pressure comparison is described. Reject times based on the atrium curve (0.10 s and 0.45 s are when the bicuspid valve closes and opens).",
     "model": "The semilunar valve opens at about 0.15 s and closes at about 0.40 s. It opens when the pressure in the left ventricle rises above the pressure in the aorta, and closes when the ventricular pressure falls below the aortic pressure, so these are the two times at which the ventricle and aorta curves cross.",
     "commonErrors": "Using the crossings of the ventricle and atrium curves (the bicuspid valve); reading the time of peak pressure."
