   "intro": "<p>The bacterium <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> lives in the layer of mucus that covers the epithelium of the human stomach. Read the information below, then answer both parts of this question in continuous prose.</p>\n<ul><li><em>H. pylori</em> is found in the stomachs of about half of all humans. Most infected people never have symptoms, but the bacterium can damage epithelial cells and releases toxins, so the stomach lining becomes inflamed.</li>\n<li>Damaged epithelial cells are replaced by mitosis, so cells in an inflamed stomach lining divide more often than usual. In 1994 the International Agency for Research on Cancer concluded that infection with <em>H. pylori</em> is carcinogenic to humans.</li>\n<li>Other factors, such as smoking and eating a lot of salt-preserved food, are also associated with stomach cancer.</li>\n<li><em>H. pylori</em> can be killed by a short course of antibiotics. Doctors are concerned that giving antibiotics to very large numbers of people could increase antibiotic resistance in bacteria.</li>\n<li>In 2014, scientists combined the results of six randomised controlled trials in healthy people infected with <em>H. pylori</em>. Five of the trials took place in East Asia and one in Colombia. The scientists described the evidence as limited and of moderate quality. They concluded that removing <em>H. pylori</em> reduces stomach cancer in infected Asian people, but said the results might not apply to other populations. Table 9 shows the combined results.</li></ul>\n<figure><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%\"><table class=\"tbl\"><caption>Table 9</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Group</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Number of people</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Number who later developed stomach cancer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Given treatment to remove <em>H. pylori</em></th><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">3294</td><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">51</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Control groups (not given this treatment)</th><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">3203</td><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">76</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figcaption class=\"small\">Real data: Ford AC et al. (2014) BMJ 348: g3174, pooled results of six randomised controlled trials. Other information from the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2005 press release and IARC Monographs volume 61 (1994).</figcaption></figure>",
