     "model": "A benign tumour grows slowly and is surrounded by a capsule, so it does not invade the tissue around it and it does not spread. A malignant tumour grows rapidly, has no capsule and invades nearby tissue; its cells can also break away and travel in the blood or lymph to form secondary tumours elsewhere, which is metastasis. A tumour forms when mutations, which are changes in the base sequence of DNA, affect the genes that control the rate of cell division. Mutations arise spontaneously during DNA replication, so cells in an inflamed stomach lining, which divide more often, have more opportunities to acquire them. Proto-oncogenes stimulate cell division, and a mutated proto-oncogene, called an oncogene, stimulates the cell to divide too quickly. Tumour suppressor genes slow cell division, and a mutation can inactivate such a gene so that the rate of division increases. Usually several of these mutations build up in one cell line before division becomes uncontrolled and a tumour forms.",
