     "model": "The information gives some support to the headline. In six randomised controlled trials, 51 of the 3294 treated people (1.5 %) developed stomach cancer, compared with 76 of the 3203 people in the control groups (2.4 %), so removing the bacterium was followed by about a third fewer cases. Because the trials were randomised and controlled, this suggests that the infection contributes to causing the cancer, which agrees with the IARC conclusion. However, the headline goes too far. Treatment did not prevent cancer, because 51 treated people still developed it, so it only reduced the risk. Five of the six trials took place in East Asia, the evidence was described as limited and of moderate quality, and the scientists said the results might not apply to other populations. Stomach cancer is also associated with other factors, such as smoking and salt-preserved food, and most of the half of all humans who carry the bacterium never develop it. Treating very large numbers of people with antibiotics could also increase antibiotic resistance. Overall, the evidence suggests that killing the bacterium reduces the risk of stomach cancer in some populations, but it does not show that it prevents cancer.",
